(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-mycobacterial-infections-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Mycobacterial Infections and Tuberculosis Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has untreated latent tuberculosis infection. She is about to begin prolonged corticosteroid therapy for another illness. Which complication is she now at higher risk of?","choices":["Healing into a scar of nonviable organisms","Progression to tuberculosis disease","Spread of her latent infection to family","A falsely positive interferon-gamma assay"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prolonged corticosteroid therapy is one of the higher progression risks, alongside human immunodeficiency virus infection, transplantation and tumor necrosis factor inhibitors. Latent infection can progress to disease, often with impaired immunity, which is the risk she now carries. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 15, 23"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man starts treatment for macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease and asks how long he will need it. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["12 months past culture conversion","12 months from the first dose","6 months after symptoms resolve","Until his chest imaging is normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment continues for at least 12 months after culture conversion, so the clock starts when cultures turn negative, not with the first dose. That makes the whole course longer than 12 months. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has a chest radiograph showing fibrotic upper-lobe scarring that suggests prior tuberculosis, and he has never been treated for it. His tuberculin skin test shows 6 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Negative; his cut-off is 15 mm","Negative; his cut-off is 10 mm","Negative; old scars cause false results","Positive; his cut-off is 5 mm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Imaging suggesting prior tuberculosis places him in the 5 mm group, together with human immunodeficiency virus, recent close contact, transplant and major immunosuppression. 6 mm meets that cut-off, and his fibrotic lesion also warrants review of prior treatment and exclusion of active disease. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 29"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis and, after expert review, is eligible for a shorter regimen. Which shorter regimen fits her?","choices":["4-month multidrug regimen for children","17-week rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen","3-month weekly isoniazid-rifapentine","4-month daily rifampin alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients aged 12 or older with susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis can receive the 17-week rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen: 8 weeks of isoniazid, rifapentine, pyrazinamide and moxifloxacin, then 9 weeks of isoniazid, rifapentine and moxifloxacin. Eligibility needs expert review. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has latent tuberculosis infection; disease has been excluded, he takes no other medicines, and the source case's organism is fully susceptible. Which is a preferred short regimen?","choices":["Rifampin daily for 4 months","Isoniazid daily for 9 months","Four drugs for 2 months, then two for 4","17-week rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Daily rifampin for 4 months is one of the preferred short latent-infection regimens, along with weekly isoniazid plus rifapentine for 3 months and daily isoniazid plus rifampin for 3 months. Isoniazid alone for 6 or 9 months is the alternative when rifamycins are unsuitable. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 37"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman shared an office with a coworker diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis 2 weeks ago, and her exposure has now ended. She has no symptoms and no immune problem, and her interferon-gamma release assay today is negative. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Three sputum specimens today","Begin latent-infection treatment","Repeat the test in 8 to 10 weeks","No further testing is needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early infection can produce a negative skin test or interferon-gamma release assay, so testing is repeated 8 to 10 weeks after the last exposure when indicated. She is symptom-free and immunocompetent, so no immediate disease evaluation is triggered. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has nonsevere drug-susceptible tuberculosis and, after expert review, is a candidate for a shorter regimen. Which shorter regimen fits him?","choices":["17-week rifapentine-moxifloxacin course","4 months of rifampin alone","3 months of weekly two-drug therapy","4 months of multidrug therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Selected children with nonsevere tuberculosis can receive 4 months: 2 months of isoniazid, rifampin and pyrazinamide, with or without ethambutol, then 2 months of isoniazid and rifampin. Eligibility, interactions and disease site need expert review. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man had calcified granulomas on chest radiographs years ago and has had no new tuberculosis exposure. After months of prolonged corticosteroid therapy for another condition, he develops cough, night sweats, weight loss and an upper-lobe cavity. Which stage of tuberculosis best fits?","choices":["Progressive primary tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis infection","Reinfection with a new strain","Reactivation (secondary) tuberculosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Calcified granulomas may reflect prior infection, and prolonged corticosteroid therapy raises progression risk; reactivation is latent infection that progresses to disease, often with impaired immunity. Post-primary disease often involves the upper lobes and may cavitate, and with no new exposure, reinfection is not suggested. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 15, 28, 29"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man has 5 weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss, and pulmonary tuberculosis is suspected. Which sputum collection plan is appropriate?","choices":["One sample, collected at any time of day","Two samples, collected 1 hour apart in clinic","Three samples, 1 each week for 3 weeks","Three samples 8 to 24 hours apart, 1 early morning"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Suspected pulmonary tuberculosis calls for 3 sputum specimens collected 8 to 24 hours apart, with at least one early-morning specimen, sent for acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, with a rapid molecular test on at least one. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with 5 weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss is told he needs evaluation for pulmonary tuberculosis. He says he cannot have tuberculosis because he has never coughed up blood. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Hemoptysis appears before other symptoms","A normal exam would rule out tuberculosis","Hemoptysis is not needed for the diagnosis","Without hemoptysis, the disease is latent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dramatic hemoptysis is not required for a diagnosis of tuberculosis. Persistent cough, fever, night sweats, weight loss, reduced appetite and fatigue are the usual manifestations, and hemoptysis or pleuritic chest pain may or may not occur. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 17, 10"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has a positive interferon-gamma release assay. She also reports 5 weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss. Which action should be avoided at this point?","choices":["Starting latent-infection treatment now","Ordering a chest radiograph","Collecting three sputum specimens","Asking about her exposure history"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tuberculosis disease must be excluded before latent-infection treatment, and a positive result requires evaluation for disease before preventive treatment. Her cough, night sweats and weight loss make disease possible, so she needs chest imaging and sputum testing first. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 21, 37"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man is diagnosed with latent tuberculosis infection after a normal clinical evaluation. He lives with his newborn daughter and asks whether he could pass the infection to her. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["He is contagious until his treatment ends","He can spread it through close household contact","He is contagious when he coughs or sings","Latent infection does not spread to others"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. People with latent tuberculosis infection are infected but asymptomatic, are not infectious and cannot spread the disease; latent infection does not cause transmission. Spread comes from infectious aerosols released by pulmonary or laryngeal disease during coughing, speaking or singing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 13"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman who rehearses with a church choir indoors for hours each week is diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis. She asks how she could have spread it to other choir members. Which describes how tuberculosis spreads in this setting?","choices":["Brief contact while outdoors","Infectious airborne aerosols","Shared cups and hymn books","Hand contact during services"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary and laryngeal tuberculosis release infectious aerosols during coughing, speaking or singing, and risk rises with prolonged shared indoor air and poor ventilation. A choir rehearsing indoors for hours fits that pattern. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has latent tuberculosis infection; disease has been excluded. Her pharmacist reports that her long-term medicines have serious interactions with rifamycins. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Isoniazid plus rifampin daily, 3 months","Isoniazid alone for 6 or 9 months","Rifampin daily for 4 months","Isoniazid plus rifapentine weekly, 3 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid for 6 or 9 months is the alternative when rifamycin regimens are unsuitable, and checking interactions is part of choosing a latent-infection regimen. Each preferred short regimen contains a rifamycin (rifampin or rifapentine), which her medicines rule out. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with presumed infectious pulmonary tuberculosis is in an airborne infection isolation room. A nurse is about to enter her room. Which respiratory protection should the nurse wear?","choices":["Gloves and gown alone","No mask if she is masked","Fit-tested N95 respirator","A standard surgical mask"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staff use fit-tested N95 or higher-level respiratory protection when caring for presumed or confirmed infectious tuberculosis. The organism spreads through aerosols released during coughing, speaking or singing, so the protection has to be respiratory. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man moved 1 year ago from an area with high tuberculosis incidence. He has 6 weeks of productive cough, fever, night sweats, weight loss and hemoptysis, and his chest radiograph shows an upper-lobe cavity. His interferon-gamma release assay is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pulmonary tuberculosis","Lung abscess","Lung cancer","Nontuberculous mycobacterial disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Residence in a high-incidence area is an exposure risk, and persistent cough, fever, night sweats, weight loss and hemoptysis are pulmonary manifestations; post-primary disease often involves the upper lobes and may cavitate. Imaging and the blood test support the suspicion; sputum microbiology confirms it. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 14, 16, 18, 28"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman in a long-term care home has 2 months of fatigue, weight loss and low-grade fever. A close contact in her facility was recently diagnosed with infectious tuberculosis. Her lung examination is normal. Which conclusion should be avoided?","choices":["Her symptoms warrant a tuberculosis workup","Imaging and sputum should guide the workup","A normal exam excludes pulmonary tuberculosis","Older adults may present atypically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Physical examination has limited sensitivity for tuberculosis, and a normal examination does not exclude pulmonary disease. Older adults may present atypically, so her symptoms, exposure history, imaging and microbiology must guide the evaluation. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man with cough and weight loss has a sputum culture that identifies Mycobacterium bovis. How is this organism classified?","choices":["A Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex member","A nontuberculous mycobacterium","A Mycobacterium avium complex member","A rapidly growing environmental species"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex causes tuberculosis; Mycobacterium tuberculosis is its principal human pathogen, and other members include Mycobacterium bovis and Mycobacterium africanum. Species identification matters because transmission and treatment differ from the nontuberculous mycobacteria. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man had a kidney transplant 2 years ago and takes anti-rejection medicines. His tuberculin skin test shows 8 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Positive; his cut-off is 5 mm","Negative; his cut-off is 10 mm","Negative; his cut-off is 15 mm","Falsely positive from his medicines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Transplant recipients are in the 5 mm group, together with human immunodeficiency virus, recent close contact, major immunosuppression and imaging suggesting prior tuberculosis. 8 mm meets 5 mm, so the result is positive; transplantation is also a higher progression risk. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 21, 22, 23"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with no known tuberculosis risk factors has his tuberculin skin test read at 48 hours. There is 20 mm of erythema around 9 mm of transverse induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Positive; 20 mm is above his 15 mm cut-off","Positive; 9 mm is above a 5 mm cut-off","Positive; 20 mm is above a 10 mm cut-off","Negative; 9 mm is below his 15 mm cut-off"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The reader measures transverse induration in millimeters and does not measure erythema. With no known risk factors his cut-off is 15 mm, and his induration is 9 mm, so the result is negative despite the wide redness. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 22"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman on targeted testing for tuberculosis infection has an interferon-gamma release assay reported as indeterminate. She has no symptoms. Which next test is most appropriate?","choices":["Three sputum specimens for culture","Repeat the assay or use a skin test","Treat as negative; no further testing","Treat as positive; start therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An indeterminate, invalid or borderline interferon-gamma release assay is handled by repeating the assay or using a tuberculin skin test as clinically appropriate. An indeterminate result says nothing either way about infection, so another immune-response test is needed. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with bronchiectasis has a year of chronic cough with sputum, fatigue and weight loss. Computed tomography shows nodular bronchiectasis, alternative diagnoses have been excluded, and two sputum cultures grow Mycobacterium avium complex. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis","Nontuberculous mycobacterial disease","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Colonization without disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mycobacterium avium complex is a common cause of nontuberculous lung disease, and bronchiectasis raises the risk. She meets every diagnostic domain: compatible symptoms and imaging (nodular bronchiectasis), exclusion of alternatives, and 2 or more sputum cultures with the same species. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 39"},{"q":"A 2-year-old girl lives with her grandmother, who was just diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis. The girl is well, and her first tuberculin skin test shows 0 mm of induration. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Reassurance that she is uninfected","Repeat testing in 1 year","The 17-week rifapentine regimen","Prompt public-health assessment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Early infection can produce a negative skin test, so one negative result soon after exposure does not settle it. Young children and immunocompromised contacts need prompt public-health assessment, and infancy and young childhood carry a higher risk of progression after infection. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 23"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has tuberculosis of a cervical lymph node, with persistent painless enlargement. Chest imaging and sputum testing show no pulmonary or laryngeal disease, and no aerosol-generating procedure is planned. Her coworkers ask whether she is contagious. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["She spreads it through skin contact","She needs airborne isolation","She is usually not infectious","She is infectious until treatment ends"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Extrapulmonary disease usually is not infectious unless there is pulmonary or laryngeal involvement or an aerosol-generating source. Her disease is confined to a lymph node, so the airborne route that spreads tuberculosis is absent. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 13"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman who received BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccine as a child has no known tuberculosis risk factors. A tuberculin skin test shows 16 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Indeterminate; repeat the skin test","Positive; her cut-off is 15 mm","Negative; the vaccine explains it","Positive; treat without further workup"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The skin test is interpreted with the standard risk-based thresholds regardless of vaccination history. With no known risk factors her cut-off is 15 mm, and 16 mm meets it, so she needs evaluation for tuberculosis disease before any preventive treatment. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 21, 22, 19, 25"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has chronic cough, sputum, fatigue and weight loss. Computed tomography shows fibrocavitary disease, alternative diagnoses have been excluded, and her sputum cultures are negative, but one bronchoalveolar lavage culture grows Mycobacterium avium complex. Which best describes whether she meets the diagnostic criteria?","choices":["Met on imaging alone","Met; one positive lavage qualifies","Not met; two sputum cultures needed","Not met; a biopsy is required"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nontuberculous lung disease requires all domains: compatible symptoms and imaging (fibrocavitary disease here), exclusion of alternatives, and microbiology. One positive bronchial wash or lavage can meet the microbiologic criterion, even when sputum is negative. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman starting work in a setting with ongoing tuberculosis transmission has a tuberculin skin test placed on her forearm. She asks when she must come back to have it read. When should a trained reader measure the reaction?","choices":["12 to 24 hours after placement","24 to 48 hours after placement","48 to 72 hours after placement","96 to 120 hours after placement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A trained reader measures the reaction at 48 to 72 hours after the intradermal injection on the forearm, recording the transverse induration in millimeters, not the erythema. She must return within that window. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection has 3 months of back pain, fever and weight loss. Imaging shows vertebral destruction, and she has new leg weakness. Spinal tuberculosis is suspected. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Interferon-gamma release assay alone","Latent-infection treatment for 4 months","Urgent assessment with spinal specimens","Repeat imaging in 3 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spinal tuberculosis presents with back pain, vertebral destruction or neurologic deficits, and extrapulmonary disease may need organ-specific specimens and urgent assessment. Her new leg weakness is a neurologic deficit, which makes the assessment urgent. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man is diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis, and the health department is ranking his contacts. Which contact has the highest exposure risk?","choices":["Office mate in a small unventilated room","Neighbor who chatted with him outdoors","Cousin he visited once for an hour","Clinic receptionist he passed briefly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exposure risk rises with close contact with a person who has infectious tuberculosis and with prolonged exposure in enclosed, poorly ventilated spaces. Sharing a small unventilated office combines both, giving the most prolonged shared indoor air. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 13, 14"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man is admitted with cough, night sweats and weight loss, and his sputum smear shows acid-fast bacilli. Infectious pulmonary tuberculosis is presumed. Where should he be placed?","choices":["Shared ward with standard precautions","Contact precautions with gown and gloves","Hallway bed until cultures return","Airborne infection isolation room"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Presumed infectious pulmonary tuberculosis calls for an airborne infection isolation room when indicated, with fit-tested N95 or higher-level respiratory protection for staff, because pulmonary disease releases infectious aerosols during coughing, speaking or singing. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 13, 33"}]};
